<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">John of Ibelin, the Old Lord of Beirut</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1179–<a href="/years/1236">1236</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-ibelin">House of Ibelin</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">John of Ibelin, the Old Lord of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beirut">Beirut</a>, was a half-brother of Queen Isabella I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> and the most powerful baron of the crusader states in his time. He received Beirut after its recovery in 1197, rebuilt its citadel and a celebrated palace, and served as regent of Jerusalem and of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a>. From 1228 he led the barons' resistance to Emperor Frederick II and his agents in the War of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombards</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>John was the son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balian-of-ibelin">Balian of Ibelin</a>, Lord of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nablus">Nablus</a> and Ibelin, and Maria Komnene, widow of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amalric-i-of-jerusalem">Amalric of Jerusalem</a>. By 1198, before he was 20, he had become constable of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>. He later gave up the office in exchange for the lordship of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beirut">Beirut</a>, a city <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saladin">Saladin</a>'s conquest had left completely destroyed. Wilbrand of Oldenburg, a German ambassador, described his new palace in 1212: frescoes, mosaic floors resembling the sea, a painted ceiling of moving skies and a marble fountain with a dragon in the central hall. In 1207 his marriage to Melisende of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arsuf">Arsuf</a> added Arsuf to his lands and made him one of the wealthiest nobles of the kingdom.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>From 1205 to 1210 John was regent in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acre">Acre</a> for his niece Maria of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montferrat">Montferrat</a>. He helped arrange the marriage of Hugh I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a> to Alice of Champagne and, in 1210, Maria's marriage to John of Brienne. His brother Philip of Ibelin was regent of Cyprus for the young Henry I of Cyprus from 1218, and on Philip's death in 1228 John took over the office. That year Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-ii">Frederick II</a> arrived on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sixth-crusade">Sixth Crusade</a>, claimed the overlordship of Cyprus and the lordship of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beirut">Beirut</a>, and forced John at a banquet ringed by armed guards to give up the regency.</p><p>After Frederick left, John's forces defeated the imperial bailiffs outside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicosia">Nicosia</a> on 14 July 1229, beginning the War of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombards</a>. In 1231 he repelled the army of the imperial marshal Richard Filangieri at the Battle of Agridi, but the imperial fleet besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beirut">Beirut</a>, whose castle held out for months. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acre">Acre</a> his supporters formed a commune and elected him mayor in 1232; the commune relieved Beirut, and on 15 June the imperial forces were beaten on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a>, where Henry I, now of age, ruled undisputed.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Filangieri still held <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyre">Tyre</a>, and neither side could prevail; in 1234 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-gregory-ix">Pope Gregory IX</a> excommunicated John and his supporters, a sentence partly revoked in 1235. In his last year John insisted, against his family's wishes, on joining the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/knights-templar">Knights Templar</a> so that he could die as a Templar, and he was given a grand funeral in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acre">Acre</a> in 1236. His son Balian succeeded him as Lord of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beirut">Beirut</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “John, Old Lord of Beirut” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its most famous lord, <b>John of Ibelin, the Old Lord of Beirut</b>, rebuilt the citadel and a palace whose marble and mosaics impressed visitors, and led the baronial resistance to the imperial officials of Frederick II in the 1230s.</blockquote><a href="/state/lordship-of-beirut">Lordship of Beirut</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in Acre, in what is now <a href="/countries/israel">Israel</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=32.93,35.08&amp;year=1216" aria-label="Acre on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5449 968 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of John of Ibelin, the Old Lord of Beirut"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5899 1238h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5899 1238h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=32.93,35.08&amp;year=1216">What was here in 1216? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
